AI Usage:
    I asked ChatGPT the following questions:
        How do I check for EOF with std::ifstream::get()?
            Answer: ifstream::get will return an EOF char if there aren't any characters left in the file.
            All I had to do was make the default case return an EOFtk when nothing else matched.
        
How to compile:
    1. make sure you are in the "Gavin-Dennis-P2/" directory
    2. in the terminal, run the "make" command to compile the programs
    3. Optionally, run the following commands to execute the program with the following
       test cases: (documentation about test cases at the bottom of this page)
    * make test - runs P2 with tests/P2_test (a file that doesn't exist) as an argument
	* make test1 - runs P2 with tests/P2_test1 as argument
	* make test1e - runs P2 with tests/P2_test1e as argument (this one has an invalid token)
	* make test2 - runs P2 with tests/P2_test2 as argument
	* make test3 - runs P2 with tests/P2_test3 as argument
	* make test4 - runs P2 with tests/P2_test4 as argument
	* make testGavin - runs P2 with tests/P2_testGavin as argument (the most comprehensive test)
    4. the finished executuble 'P2' can be found in the 'build' directory (Gavin-Dennis-P2/build/)

Problems encountered:
    Line number issue: resolved 2025-04-11
        I thought I was having trouble getting ifstream::get to parse new lines in order to get the
        correct line number. As it turns out, my file filter function in main (which removes comments
        from the original input file) wasn't adding a new line character at the end of each line. As
        a result, the filtered file would be one long line.

Test cases:
    P2_test1.txt - based on example 1 from assignment specifications. A is empty.
    P2_test1e.txt - based on test1, but is invalid so it should throw an error. Should be a parsing error and not a scanning error.
    P2_test2.txt - based on example 2 from assignment specifications. A has a value this time.
    P2_test3.txt - based on example 3 from assignment specifications. The first B nonterminal resolves to another S nonterminal, making it much longer.
    P2_test4.txt - based on example 4 from assignment specifications. Only one S nonterminal and no comments, but still a complicated test.
	P2_testGavin - my personal test case. The longest and most complicated, containing extra spaces and lines, multiple comments, and three S nonterminals.